WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.440
Float on down.

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:05.600
We're going deep into the sewers to dig up
all the Easter eggs and references you might've

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.220
missed in It: Chapter Two.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.800
Now is your last chance to turn back, because
there are plenty of spoilers and scares ahead.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.460
Stephen King's stories are often connected
through their New England locations as well

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.420
as their monsters.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.200
It takes place in Derry, Maine, and the book
often references the nearby town of Castle

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:26.630
Rock, where multiple works by King are set,
including his novella "The Body" and its movie

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.000
adaptation, Stand By Me.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.529
These two stories are also connected through
their focus on friendship, childhood trauma,

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:36.489
and the end of innocence as groups of kids
are forced to confront dark forces around

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:38.100
them, both supernatural and human.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.930
At the end of Stand By Me, Gordie LaChance
is finishing the last lines of his memoir,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.560
inspired by the news of his childhood best
friend's death.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.860
His office has dark wood paneling and a wall
of books around him, as well as a picture

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.680
window looking out onto the front yard.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:57.090
In It: Chapter Two, Bill's office is a replica
of Gordie's, albeit with a sleek laptop rather

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.200
than a clunky 1980s home computer.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.000
And like in Stand By Me, Bill is working on
his own book about childhood.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.080
1986's Stand By Me was the film that gave
Stephen King a reputation for more than his

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:09.660
horror stories.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.220
It also helped catapult young actors River
Phoenix, Wil Wheaton, Corey Feldman, and Jerry

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.500
O'Connell to stardom in film and television.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.540
Phoenix's promising career was sadly cut short
in its prime when he died of an overdose in

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.540
1993.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.280
In It: Chapter Two, the film adaptation of
Bill's horror novel is being helmed by Peter

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.350
Bogdanovich, who directed River Phoenix in
one of his final movies, The Thing Called

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:31.840
Love.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.740
As it turns out, cinema legend Bogdanovich
is actually a friend of It director Andy Muschietti

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.260
and asked for the cameo personally.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:43.439
The connection to Stand By Me and River Phoenix
is the poetry of coincidence, but still brings

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.200
a melancholy touch to It: Chapter Two for
fans who remember him fondly.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.040
In Stephen King's epic gunslinger fantasy
series The Dark Tower, roses have a special

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:53.760
significance.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.079
Their appearance often indicates a nearby
portal to parallel universes, and certain

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.660
roses are powerful enough to enchant and heal
with their mystical properties.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.460
One in particular, known simply as The Rose,
is found in an abandoned lot in New York City

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.780
at the corner of Second and Forty-Sixth street.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.660
Rose symbolism appears frequently in King's
works, including It.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.020
In It: Chapter Two, Bill finds his old bike
Silver at the vintage shop Secondhand Rose,

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.050
which is a sly reference to things that were
once beautiful and important but are now merely

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.420
taking up shelf space in a small-town store.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.380
In the book, the store has the full name Secondhand
Rose, Secondhand Clothes, and Bill buys his

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.800
Schwinn for $20.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.060
In Chapter Two the proprietor, played by Stephen
King himself, charges Bill $300.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.460
When the older Losers go back to the house
on Neibolt Street in Chapter Two, a 1950s-era

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.280
Amana fridge that was first seen in Chapter
One makes another appearance.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.680
Inside the fridge they find Stanley's decapitated
head, along with a host of other nastiness.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.720
In the novel It, that fridge played heavily
in a number of disturbing scenes that have

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.390
never made it to the screen due to their graphic
nature.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.060
Most notably, the fridge in the junkyard is
where Pennywise victim and school bully Patrick

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.560
Hockstetter would trap animals and wait for
them to suffocate to death.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.380
It's also the site where Patrick met his untimely
end in a swarm of flying leeches.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.240
"Mother f------------"

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.680
In It: Chapter Two, the civic pride expressed
by the phrase "I Heart Derry" takes a sinister

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.180
turn.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.240
In Chapter One, the message can be seen printed
on one of Pennywise's red balloons, a reference

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.360
to a horrific hate crime in the book that
hadn't been put to screen until Chapter Two.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.070
After winning an "I Heart Derry" cap at the
Canal Days carnival, Adrian Mellon and his

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.880
boyfriend begin walking home, talking about
their future.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:43.900
On the way, they are set upon by a group of
homophobic bullies who viciously beat the

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.540
couple, throwing Adrian's body over the bridge
and into the speeding current below, where

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.820
Pennywise proceeds to eat him.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.220
King based Adrian Mellon's storyline on a
real-life hate crime that played out exactly

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:54.940
as he wrote it.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.840
Minus the cannibal clown, at least.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.540
It: Chapter Two is the first adaptation to
put these horrifying events on screen, putting

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.900
the previous "I Heart Derry" reference into
its proper context.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.880
In every It adaptation, and as per the book,
the local cinema plays a huge part in the

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:11.460
Losers' story.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.560
During the summer it's a safe space for kids
to escape from Henry Bowers and his gang of

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.060
bullies.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.860
It's also the place where many of Pennywise's
tormenting monsters take shape for the Losers,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.080
like Ritchie's Wolf Man.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.460
But in It: Chapter Two, the movie theater
is boarded up and closed down, with only movie

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.540
posters to indicate when the shuttering happened.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.780
We get a closer look when Richie visits the
place during his search for a token, and one

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:38.250
of the posters is 1998's Tom Hanks and Meg
Ryan rom-com You've Got Mail, which implies

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.400
that the theater closed its doors sometime
before the turn of the century.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.920
But more ominously, there are two rips in
the poster that spell out a scraggly "IT."

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:50.680
Turns out, that's a sneaky bit of foreshadowing,
since Richie is about to have his own run-in

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:51.680
with Pennywise.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.420
"Beep beep, Richie"

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.280
In the novel, the Bradley Gang were a group
of gangsters who made Derry a pit-stop during

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:03.800
Prohibition.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:08.480
One day, in a strange series of events that
points to Pennywise's evil machinations, the

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.700
Bradley Gang were massacred by townsfolk in
one fell swoop.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.220
The event went down as historic legend, and
both It: Chapter One and Chapter Two feature

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.780
a mural in Derry commemorating the gruesome
event.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.480
What Chapter Two adds to the story is that
Ben actually found the Bradley Gang's bunker

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.510
and reinforced it so the Losers could use
it as a hideout from Henry Bowers and the

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.240
other bullies.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.460
This is why Ben finds a bunch of rusted old
padlocks and chains down there.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.392
The Bradley Gang had already made this a secure
hiding spot, and the hole was waiting in the

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.280
woods for the Losers to find and adopt it
as their own.

